---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import { ambitions, sunriseTimeline } from '../data/content';
import { href, sources, asset, site } from '../data/site';
---
<Layout title="Project SUNRISE" current="projects" description="Explore Project SUNRISE: a founding community, a modular rover vision, and power-generation research at Penn State University Park.">
  <section class="hero project-hero"><img class="hero-image" src={asset('images/mars-sunrise.webp')} alt="" width="2164" height="727" fetchpriority="high" /><div class="container hero-content"><h1>Project SUNRISE</h1><p class="hero-description">Exploring modular rover architecture<br />for different mission profiles.</p></div></section>
<section class="container project-overview"><dl class="project-facts"><div><dt>Rover</dt><dd>SUNRISE</dd></div><div><dt>Current stage</dt><dd>Concept stage</dd></div><div><dt>Operational deployment</dt><dd>{site.target}<small class="fact-note">Our internal goal for completing the final prototype. Development prototypes and testing are planned well before then.</small></dd></div></dl><div class="split"><h2>Project overview</h2><p>SUNRISE is our proposed rover. Project SUNRISE is the development effort: establishing a space-focused engineering community, investigating a modular rover platform, and exploring conventional power-generation methods beyond photovoltaic (PV) solar and radioisotope thermoelectric generators (RTGs).</p></div></section>
  <section class="container article-section"><div class="ambition-list">{ambitions.map(item => <article><span class="index">{item.number} / OUR AMBITION</span><h2>{item.title}</h2><p>{item.text}</p></article>)}</div></section>
  <section class="container article-section split"><div><h2>Why SUNRISE?</h2></div><div><p>For Nittany Rover Team, SUNRISE represents the beginning of a more active community of people pursuing careers in space. It also expresses our ambition to explore rover architectures that can adapt to different mission profiles.</p><p>In March 2009, NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory announced that SUNRISE was one of nine finalist names in the public poll for the Mars Science Laboratory rover. NASA’s official account identifies the contest as a nationwide student essay effort, but does not identify the student who submitted SUNRISE. We therefore credit the finalist record without attributing that proposal to an unverified person.</p><p>The competition’s outcome was <em>Curiosity</em>, the name proposed by sixth-grader Clara Ma for the Mars Science Laboratory rover. SUNRISE is our own project name, inspired by that moment when students were invited to help name a Mars rover.</p><p class="source-note">Sources: <a href={sources.namingNASA}>NASA/JPL finalist announcement</a> · <a href={sources.curiosity}>NASA/JPL naming announcement</a> · <a href={sources.naming}>contemporary finalist report</a></p></div></section>
  <section class="container article-section"><p class="eyebrow">From concept to field testing</p><h2>SUNRISE development timeline</h2><p>SUNRISE is at the concept stage. This proposed path follows the rover through design, construction, and testing. Development prototypes and testing are planned well before our {site.target} operational deployment milestone. Individual milestone dates remain unset, and testing will inform each design iteration.</p><ol class="timeline">{sunriseTimeline.map((step, i) => <li class:list={{current:i===0}}><span>0{i+1} / {i===0 ? 'CURRENT — CONCEPT' : 'PLANNED'}</span><div class="timeline-detail"><h3>{step.title}</h3><p>{step.description}</p></div></li>)}</ol></section>
  <section class="container section split"><h2>Join the founding team</h2><div><p>We’re looking for experienced University Park students who can turn a shared vision into careful design, practical experiments, and documented progress.</p><a class="button" href={href('join')}>Explore joining the team ↗</a></div></section>
</Layout>
